  "## Question

ffmpeg is roughly 1.7M lines. The claim to test is that a large part of that
volume is each codec re-stating table-driven bitstream structure as bespoke
procedural code, and that if the structure were DATA one generic reader would
interpret it — N codecs costing O(N) data and O(1) code instead of O(N) code.

ISO/IEC 14496-10 §7.3 does print H.264 syntax as a table: rows of (element
name, descriptor, presence condition) with descriptors u(n), ue(v), se(v),
f(n), ae(v). This ADR tests whether the hand-written parsers in this repo are
in fact transcriptions of that table, and lands a table-driven reader ALONGSIDE
them — never replacing them — with an equivalence test, so the design is judged
on evidence.

## Measurement (the answer is: partly, and the qualification matters more)

Every non-blank, non-comment line of h264.sps/parse, h264.sps/skip-scaling-list!,
h264.pps/parse and h264.slice/parse-header! was classified. Line-by-line
assignments are in docs/measurement/syntax-table-compression.edn so the ratio is
re-checkable rather than asserted.

  152 lines total
   79 (52.0%) direct §7.3 syntax-table transcription
   16 (10.5%) reader scaffolding the generic reader subsumes
   57 (37.5%) genuine logic

So 62.5% of these parsers is compressible into data plus one shared reader.
That supports the thesis at the level it was aimed.

The 37.5% that is NOT table divides into three unequal parts, and the division
is the useful finding:

   31 lines (54% of the logic) — the OUTPUT MAP. Renaming frame_mbs_only_flag
      to :frame-mbs-only?, folding pic_init_qp_minus26 + 26 into :pic-init-qp,
      mapping entropy_coding_mode_flag to :cabac / :cavlc. This is the shim
      between the standard's names and this repo's public API. A generic reader
      emits a map keyed by the spec name for free, so a good part of this would
      collapse into a declarative rename table too. It is counted as logic here
      because this branch did not build one.
   15 lines (26%) — DELIBERATE REFUSALS. `throw` on MMCO, on weighted
      prediction, on reference-list reordering, on FMO, on pic_order_cnt_type 1
      in the slice header. A table over the FULL §7.3.3 would not need these:
      they are logic this repo chose, not logic the table lacks.
   11 lines (19%) — §7.4 DERIVATIONS. SubWidthC / SubHeightC / CropUnitX /
      CropUnitY and the width and height they produce; the Table 7-6 mod-5
      slice_type collapse; the lastScale/nextScale state machine inside
      §7.3.2.1.1.1 scaling_list.

Only that last 11 lines is irreducible. It is small, and it is exactly what the
standard itself puts in a different clause.

### The number that qualifies the thesis

src/h264/*.cljc is 6,454 lines. sps + pps + slice together are 490 of them
(7.6%); the parse bodies measured above are 152 (2.4%). The other 5,964 lines
are decode, encode, cabac, cavlc, interp, transform, intra_pred, quant — §8
reconstruction and §9 entropy coding. None of it is a §7.3 syntax table.
decode.cljc has 14 raw Exp-Golomb read sites in 1,683 lines, against 28 CABAC
and 4 CAVLC call sites.

macroblock_layer (§7.3.5) IS printed as a syntax table, and is the largest one
in the standard. It still resists this format, for two reasons that are not
incidental: its presence conditions depend on values (mb_type to NumMbPart,
coded_block_pattern to which residual blocks are present) whose meaning is
defined in §7.4 and §9 rather than in the table, and its descriptors become
ae(v) the moment CABAC is on.

So within ONE codec the compression is real but narrow. Its value is across N
codecs: the parameter-set and header parser is the part every new codec must
write from scratch, and it is the part this format deletes.

## What was built

  src/h264/syntax.cljk                   204 lines  generic reader, O(1)
  resources/h264/syntax/sps.edn           95 lines  §7.3.2.1.1 as DATA, O(N)
                                                    63 non-comment, 29 rows
  src/h264/sps_table.cljk                103 lines  §7.4 semantics + escape
  test/h264/syntax_equivalence_test.cljk  292 lines  equivalence vs incumbent

h264.sps, h264.pps and h264.slice are UNCHANGED. They pass real golden-vector
tests against ffmpeg output and remain the shipping parsers. Deleting working,
tested code to make an architectural point is the failure mode this branch was
built to avoid.

## The table format

Rows:

    [:u  n-expr name]        u(n), MSB first
    [:f  n-expr name]        f(n), read as u(n)
    [:ue name]               ue(v)
    [:se name]               se(v)
    [:infer name expr]       §7.4 when-not-present-infer-X; reads no bits
    [:when cond rows]        / [:when cond then-rows else-rows]
    [:for count-expr rows]   loop index available to expressions as [:idx]
    [:escape key & args]     hand off to host code
    [:unsupported reason]    reaching this row throws

`name` is a keyword, or [:vec kw] for the standard's element[ i ] array form.

Expressions are a closed grammar over already-read elements: literals,
[:var k], [:at k i], [:idx], [:has? k], = / not= / < / <= / > / :in / :and /
:or / :not, + / - / * and [:if c a b]. [:var] on an element not yet read
THROWS; it does not see nil. Presence conditions must evaluate to a strict
boolean, so a table that writes [:var :some_flag] instead of
[:= [:var :some_flag] 1] is rejected rather than silently taking the 0 branch
as true. Both refusals are tested.

Cross-structure references work by seeding the value map: §7.3.3 reads
frame_num as u(log2_max_frame_num_minus4 + 4), and that width lives in the
referenced SPS. read-structure takes a `context` map for exactly this. Tested
with a synthetic table; slice_header itself has NOT been transcribed.

## What the format cannot express, stated as limits rather than as future work

  - ae(v). An arithmetic-coded element's bit cost depends on adaptive context
    state that depends on every previously decoded element. There is no
    (name, descriptor, condition) triple that determines it. The only honest
    row is [:unsupported :ae], and reaching it throws. This is not a gap to be
    closed later; it is the boundary of the idea.
  - Sub-structures whose spec body contains ASSIGNMENTS rather than descriptor
    rows. §7.3.2.1.1.1 scaling_list is the canonical case: the loop's
    continuation depends on a running lastScale/nextScale the loop computes.
    Those take [:escape] — 8 lines of ordinary code, honestly labelled.
  - §7.4 derivations beyond a plain inferred constant.
  - more_rbsp_data()-gated tails, which need the RBSP's exact trailing-bit
    position rather than any property of the table.
  - Byte-offset arithmetic, little-endian fields and length-prefixed recursive
    containers (see reach, below).

## Evidence

Equivalence, not mere execution. `kbb -M:test`:

    before this branch  124 tests  7,077 assertions  0 failures  exit 0
    after               135 tests  7,263 assertions  0 failures  exit 0
    the new namespace    11 tests    186 assertions  0 failures  exit 0

Compared against h264.sps/parse, whole map, field for field:

  1. 27 SPS NALs across every .h264 fixture in resources/h264/fixtures,
     including sample.h264, the libx264 baseline NAL sps_test.clj pins to
     ffprobe. The count is printed and asserted positive, so a scan that found
     nothing cannot pass as clean.
  2. 90 SPSs spanning everything h264.sps/encode can produce.
  3. Synthetic streams written bit-by-bit that reach paths the incumbent's own
     tests never do: profile 100 with all eight 4:2:0 scaling lists; the
     nextScale-reaches-zero early-exit branch of scaling_list mixed with
     full-length and absent lists; chroma_format_idc 3 with
     separate_colour_plane_flag and twelve lists; pic_order_cnt_type 1 with
     num_ref_frames_in_pic_order_cnt_cycle of 0, 1, 2 and 11;
     pic_order_cnt_type 2; frame_mbs_only_flag 0 with
     mb_adaptive_frame_field_flag both ways; frame cropping in every direction
     on both chroma paths. For each of these the test also asserts the reader
     stopped at EXACTLY the bit the writer stopped at — a position check the
     incumbent cannot supply, because it owns a private reader.

The equivalence test was shown to FAIL for the reason it names, three ways,
each at the right granularity:

    reserved_zero_2bits u(2) changed to u(1)   -> 14 failures, 4 errors
    per-i scaling-list gate removed            -> 1 error, ONLY in the
                                                  high-profile test; every
                                                  other test stayed green
    poc cycle loop bound made count - 1        -> 6 failures, ONLY in the
                                                  pic_order_cnt_type 1 test

The table was restored byte-identical afterwards.

## An observation the split surfaced

Separating §7.3 from §7.4 made one §7.4 gap visible that was invisible while
the two were interleaved. §7.4.2.1.1 says CropUnitX = 1 and
CropUnitY = 2 - frame_mbs_only_flag when ChromaArrayType is 0 (monochrome, or
separate_colour_plane_flag set). h264.sps/parse computes CropUnitX = 2 there.
h264.sps-table/semantics reproduces the incumbent EXACTLY, divergence included,
because equivalence with the incumbent is the claim under test and silently
correcting the incumbent inside the challenger would destroy the only evidence
this branch exists to produce. The divergence affects cropped monochrome or
separate-colour-plane high-profile streams; this repo has no such fixture. It
is recorded here, not fixed here.

## Reach across this ecosystem's spec repos

Measured, not asserted: all seven media spec repos named below are checked out
in the superproject and were surveyed by file.

  org-aomedia-av1     7,780 src lines. sequence_header (396) + frame_header
                      (1,181) = 1,577 lines, 20% of the repo, whose own
                      docstring says «syntax transcribed from
                      06.bitstream.syntax.md ... Field order below matches the
                      syntax table exactly». The code is br/f reads chained
                      through explicitly threaded reader values (r, r1, r2 …)
                      with conditions on prior fields and a large output map —
                      the same shape as h264.sps/parse, and with MORE
                      scaffolding, since the immutable reader must be threaded
                      by hand. STRONGEST fit in the set. tables.cljc (1,775
                      lines) is already pure data, which makes the same point
                      from the other side. decode_block, transform and
                      intra_pred are DSP and are out of reach. AV1's bool
                      coder is the ae(v) problem again.
  org-iso-aac         2,656 lines. adts (163) + ics (243) are header and
                      individual_channel_stream syntax, about 15%. huffman,
                      quant, mdct, imdct are DSP.
  org-ietf-opus         945 lines. toc (36) + packet (84), about 13%. celt,
                      pvq and mdct are DSP; range_decoder is the ae(v) problem.
  org-xiph-flac       1,017 lines. STREAMINFO, metadata blocks and the frame
                      header are structure; but they are little-endian
                      byte-offset reads (u32le), not MSB-first bit rows, so
                      they need a descriptor this format does not have.
                      read-residual, restore-linear and read-subframe are Rice
                      coding and LPC — DSP.
  org-xiph-ogg          475 lines. The page header is a FIXED byte layout, not
                      a conditional syntax table. This format can express it,
                      but a plain struct description would do as well; the
                      conditional machinery earns nothing.
  org-iso-isobmff       699 lines. RESISTS, and interestingly. Nearly the whole
                      format is structure — but length-prefixed, FOURCC-typed,
                      RECURSIVE containers with 64-bit largesize and
                      size-extends-to-EOF cases and FullBox version/flags
                      prefixes. That is a different shape of structure, needing
                      a box grammar with recursion and length delimiting, not a
                      row list.
  org-microsoft-riff    427 lines. Same shape as ISOBMFF, plus little-endian.
                      RESISTS for the same reason.

The pattern: this format reaches formats whose headers are conditional,
bit-packed, MSB-first syntax tables — H.264, H.265, AV1, AAC, Opus packet
framing. Its reach is HIGHEST where the codec has the most conditional header
and lowest where the format is a recursive container or where the entropy coder
is arithmetic. Two extensions would widen it materially and are NOT taken here:
a little-endian byte-aligned descriptor set, and a recursive length-prefixed
box grammar. Both are separate decisions with their own evidence.

## Decision

  1. Keep h264.sps, h264.pps and h264.slice as the shipping parsers. Nothing
     is replaced on the strength of this branch.
  2. h264.syntax + resources/h264/syntax/*.edn are added as a tested,
     equivalence-checked alternative, so a later decision to migrate has
     evidence rather than an argument.
  3. Any future table must state, in the table itself, where it is truncated
     and where it escapes. resources/h264/syntax/sps.edn carries
     :syntax/truncated-at and names its one escape.
  4. Do not extend this format to ae(v). If a future reader needs CABAC
     elements it needs a different mechanism, not a bigger table.
  5. Before transcribing a second structure, transcribe §7.3.2.2 PPS: it is
     the cheapest next test of the format and the measurement predicts the
     WORST table share of the three (48%), so it is the honest next data
     point rather than the flattering one.

## Dependency note: the .kotoba backends

The .kotoba counterparts in this repo (src/h264/sps.kotoba, expgolomb.kotoba,
rbsp.kotoba) currently run only on the KIR reference interpreter. Measured
2026-08-29 by the requesting session: `bin/amu compile --target aarch64` is
REJECTED with :kotoba/target-rejected (the native backend admits only
word-typed scalars, no vectors) and --target wasm32 returns an internal
compiler error. Nothing in this ADR is designed around a native backend that
cannot host it today. Worth recording for when it can: the syntax table is
already a plain value, and the generic reader is a fold over rows — the shape
that a word-typed backend admits LAST is the table, because it is a nested
heterogeneous vector. That is a dependency on kotoba-lang/kotoba-native and
kotoba-lang/amu, not on anything here."}]
